I have a complete picture of the owner identity data flow. Here is my thorough report.

## Package structure

All owner-identity logic lives under `org.springframework.samples.petclinic`:
- `service/` — the derivation policies (the bulk of the logic)
- `mapper/OwnerMapper.java` — assembles the response DTO from policies
- `model/Owner.java` — persistent entity holding stored ids
- `rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java` — create/read flow orchestration
- `rest/dto/OwnerDto` — generated from `src/main/resources/openapi.yml`
- `test/.../acceptance/` — black-box acceptance tests

## 1. Core derivation methods

### identityKey — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/OwnerIdentityPolicy.java`
`OwnerIdentityPolicy.identityKey(Owner)` (lines 45-49). SHA-256 hex (first 32 bytes) of `normalizedTelephone|lowerEmail|soundex(lastName)`:
```java
public static String identityKey(Owner owner) {
    return Sha256Hex.lower(OwnerFieldNormalizer.telephone(owner.getTelephone()) + "|"
        + OwnerFieldNormalizer.email(owner.getEmail()) + "|"
        + Soundex.of(owner.getLastName()), 32);
}
```
`rejectDuplicateIdentity(...)` (lines 33-42) rejects on exact key match, else delegates soft-match flagging to `OwnerPossibleDuplicatePolicy`. `DuplicateIdentityException` -> HTTP 409.

Note: the code uses `telephone|email|soundex(lastName)`, but the openapi.yml doc (lines 2182-2189) describes it as `normalizedTelephone|email|householdId`. The doc and implementation disagree.

### householdId — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/OwnerHouseholdPolicy.java`
`householdId(String lastName, String postcode)` (lines 37-39): SHA-256 lower hex, first 6 bytes (12 hex chars) of `normalizedLastName|postcode`:
```java
private static String householdId(String lastName, String postcode) {
    return Sha256Hex.lower(lastName + "|" + postcode, 6);
}
```
Assigned in `rejectDuplicateHousehold(...)` (lines 31-34) — despite the name, the method no longer rejects; it only sets the id. A second, alternative household derivation exists in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/SharedHouseholdIdAssignment.java` `assign(...)`/`householdId(lastName, address)` (lines 17-36), keyed on `lastName|address` (8 bytes), used only when `sharesHousehold==true` and a name+address match exists. Note: `SharedHouseholdIdAssignment` is not called from the current create flow (only `OwnerHouseholdPolicy` is), so it is effectively dead code on the main path.

### memberId + region code — `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/OwnerMemberIdPolicy.java`
`assignMemberId(...)` (lines 33-36) builds `<REGION><FY><HASH8><CHK>`:
```java
public static void assignMemberId(ClinicService clinicService, Owner owner) {
    String body = region(owner.getPostcode()) + fiscalYear(owner) + hash8(owner);
    owner.setMemberId(deduplicate(clinicService, body + luhn(body)));
}
```
Region-code derivation `region(String postcode)` (lines 52-63) — **postcode-range based** (NSW 2000-2099, VIC 3000-3099, QLD 4000-4099, else `UNKNOWN`); ranges in `REGION_RANGE` (lines 21-22):
```java
static String region(String postcode) {
    if (postcode != null) {
        int code = Integer.parseInt(postcode);
        for (Map.Entry<String, int[]> entry : REGION_RANGE.entrySet()) {
            int[] range = entry.getValue();
            if (code >= range[0] && code <= range[1]) {
                return entry.getKey();
            }
        }
    }
    return "UNKNOWN";
